<Layout title="Security - QuotaCanary" description="How QuotaCanary for Mac handles vendor keys and update checks." path="/security">
  <article class="page page-narrow wrap">
    <h1>Security</h1>
    <p class="eyebrow page-meta">Last updated 29 October 2026.</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The Mac app</h2>
<p>{MAC.securityParagraph}</p>
<p>{UPDATE_DISCLOSURE}</p>
<p>The Mac app has no analytics. Checks and alerts require the app running and your Mac awake and online. Local storage does not protect against someone controlling your unlocked Mac.</p>
<h2>Verify it yourself</h2>
<p>Three checks you can run without taking our word for it.</p>
<h3>1. Check the download</h3>
<p>In Terminal, hash the disk image you downloaded and compare it with the <a href={MAC_RELEASE_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{MAC_TAG} release</a>. The expected value is:</p>
<pre><code>{MAC_DMG_SHA256}</code></pre>
<pre><code>shasum -a 256 ~/Downloads/{MAC_DMG_FILE}</code></pre>
<h3>2. Watch what it sends</h3>
<p>With the app running, list its network connections:</p>
<pre><code>nettop -p QuotaCanary -m tcp</code></pre>
<p>It shows remote IP addresses, not names. Expect silence between checks (the app checks about every {MAC_POLL_MINUTES} minutes). When it checks, you should see connections only to the vendors of the tools you added and, if you opted in to update checks, to quotacanary.com. Resolve a name to compare, for example <code>dig +short api.apify.com</code>. A firewall such as Little Snitch or LuLu shows hostnames directly.</p>
<h3>3. Read the code</h3>
<p>The two files that hold and send your key, pinned to the {MAC_TAG} release rather than the main branch: <a href={KEYCHAIN_CODE_URL_AT_TAG} target="_blank" rel="noopener">CredentialStore.swift</a> (the Keychain store) and <a href={TRANSPORT_CODE_URL_AT_TAG} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Transport.swift</a> (the vendor requests and the redirect policy).</p>
<h2>Local data and deletion</h2>
<p>Connection settings and balance history live in the app’s local storage. Remove a connection to remove its stored key and history, or use Erase local data to remove all app-owned credentials and local files. Uninstalling alone does not erase that data.</p>
<h2>Self-hosting</h2>
<p>Not on a Mac? Run the web version yourself. You operate its server and database, and its server can decrypt the vendor keys it stores. Encryption at rest does not protect against someone with both the database and the server secrets. <a href={SELF_HOST_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the self-host guide</a>.</p>
<h2>Limit access</h2>
<p>Use read-only or narrowly scoped credentials where a vendor supports them. Some adapters put credentials in URL parameters, which may appear in vendor or proxy logs even over HTTPS.</p>
<h2>Reporting a vulnerability</h2>
<p>Email <a href="mailto:hey@quotacanary.com">hey@quotacanary.com</a> before posting a security report publicly.</p>
    </div>
